  "prompt_text": "The figure illustrates lithium dendrite formation in a lithium-metal battery and how increasing temperature changes the morphology of deposited lithium. At the top, a simplified cell stack shows a lithium anode on one side, a separator or electrolyte layer in the middle, and a cathode on the other. Blue arrows labeled Li+ indicate lithium ions moving through the electrolyte toward the anode. Gray, jagged metallic protrusions extend from the anode into the separator, representing dendrites that can grow across the cell and potentially cause short circuits.\n\nThe lower schematic focuses on the lithium anode surface and compares lithium deposits formed at different temperatures. Along a left-to-right temperature gradient, the deposits evolve from fine, branched, tree-like dendrites at lower temperature to thicker, denser, more blocky or column-like structures at higher temperature. The blue layer above the anode appears to represent the electrolyte or interfacial region where deposition occurs. Overall, the main message is that lithium deposition is strongly temperature dependent: low temperatures favor highly ramified dendritic growth, while higher temperatures seem to promote coarser, more compact lithium structures that are less filamentary.",
